// HARD Question Bank - Domain 4.0 (part A: 4.1-4.4)
window.HARD_BANK = (window.HARD_BANK || []).concat([
// ---------- 4.1 (14) ----------
{o:"4.1",q:"A company builds a CIS-based baseline and pushes it to all servers with Group Policy. Six months later an audit finds 30% of servers deviate from it, and nobody was alerted when the settings changed. Which lifecycle step failed?",c:["Establish: choosing settings from the benchmark per role", "Deploy: pushing the settings out through Group Policy", "Maintain: monitoring configurations and correcting drift", "Retire: withdrawing outdated baselines when versions change"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Establish covers selecting and documenting settings, and a CIS-based baseline was clearly produced. This would be the failing step only if the settings themselves were wrong or missing for the server role, not if they drifted afterward.", "Incorrect. Deployment obviously occurred, since most servers still match the baseline. It would be the failing step if the settings had never reached a set of servers, for example because of a missing GPO link or OU scope.", "Correct. The baseline was created and deployed successfully; what is missing is continuous compliance checking and remediation of drift, which is exactly the maintain phase. Nobody was alerted, so no monitoring or enforcement existed.", "Incorrect. Retiring a baseline is about replacing it with a newer benchmark version. Nothing in the scenario suggests the benchmark is obsolete; the problem is that live servers wander from it unnoticed."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A hospital buys one standard tablet model and issues it to nurses on each ward. IT requires full control, no personal apps or accounts of any kind, and the ability to erase the entire device if it is lost. Which model and control fit?",c:["COBO with MDM and full-device remote wipe", "COPE with MDM and a separate personal profile", "BYOD with an MDM-managed work container", "CYOD with MDM and a nurse-selected model"],a:0,e:["Correct. Corporate-owned, business-only (COBO) devices permit total control and no personal use, so a full-device wipe carries no privacy concern. The single standard model also matches COBO issuance.", "Incorrect. COPE is corporate-owned but explicitly allows personal use through a personal profile, which the hospital prohibits. It would fit if staff were allowed to keep personal apps on the same device.", "Incorrect. BYOD devices are owned by the employee, so IT cannot enforce a full-device wipe or ban personal use. It would fit if nurses used their own phones and IT managed only a work profile.", "Incorrect. CYOD lets users pick from an approved list, but the hospital buys a single standard model, so no choice exists. It would fit if nurses could choose among several approved corporate devices."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"An employee who used a personally owned phone under the BYOD program resigns. The phone has the corporate MDM work profile installed and also holds the employee's personal photos and messages. What should IT do on the last day?",c:["Full factory-reset wipe through the MDM console", "Remote lock with a new passcode set by IT", "Retire the device, revoking certificates only", "Selective wipe of the managed work profile"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A full wipe erases personal photos and messages on a device the company does not own, creating legal and HR risk. It is right for a lost or stolen corporate-owned device.", "Incorrect. Locking only stops casual access; corporate data stays on the phone and remains recoverable. It is appropriate for a device that is temporarily missing and may be found.", "Incorrect. Revoking certificates stops network access, yet cached email and documents remain on the phone. It would suffice only if no corporate data were stored locally.", "Correct. A selective (enterprise) wipe removes corporate apps and data while leaving the personal content of a device the company does not own, limiting legal and privacy exposure."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A warehouse uses handheld barcode scanners that support only WPA2 with TKIP and cannot be upgraded this year. The rest of the corporate network uses WPA2/WPA3 with AES. Which approach is BEST?",c:["WPA2 mixed mode (TKIP plus AES) on the main corporate SSID", "A TKIP-only SSID on an isolated VLAN with ACLs; others stay AES", "WPA3 transition mode (SAE plus AES) on the main corporate SSID", "Hidden SSID with MAC filtering on the main corporate network"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Mixed mode lets the corporate SSID negotiate TKIP, exposing all clients to its weaknesses. It would be acceptable only on a network that holds nothing but legacy devices.", "Correct. Containing the legacy devices on their own SSID and VLAN limits exposure while every other network keeps strong encryption until the scanners are replaced.", "Incorrect. Transition mode supports WPA2-AES and WPA3 clients but not TKIP-only devices, so the scanners still could not join. It fits mixed WPA2-AES and WPA3 client populations.", "Incorrect. Hiding an SSID and filtering MACs adds no real protection, since both are easily bypassed, and it does nothing about TKIP. It is at best a minor obstacle layered on a sound design."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A small office with no RADIUS server protects its Wi-Fi with a shared passphrase. Which WPA3 feature MOST directly protects against an attacker who captures the handshake and cracks the passphrase offline?",c:["OWE (Opportunistic Wireless Encryption)", "SAE (Dragonfly) in WPA3-Personal", "PMF (802.11w Protected Management Frames)", "AES-CCMP in WPA2-Personal"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. OWE encrypts traffic on open networks that have no password, so there is no passphrase to crack. It fits guest or hotspot networks that need encryption without credentials.", "Correct. Simultaneous Authentication of Equals prevents offline dictionary attacks on a captured handshake because every guess requires live interaction, and it adds forward secrecy.", "Incorrect. PMF protects deauthentication and disassociation frames from spoofing. It does not change how the passphrase is verified. It fits when forced-disconnect attacks are the concern.", "Incorrect. CCMP encrypts the data frames, but WPA2-Personal's four-way handshake can still be captured and attacked offline. It would be the answer for a question about data confidentiality, not handshake cracking."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A wireless survey with a heat map shows the corporate SSID is strongly receivable in the public parking lot. The network already uses WPA3-Enterprise with EAP-TLS. What is the BEST response?",c:["Add a captive portal to the corporate SSID", "Hide the SSID and turn on MAC filtering", "Switch to WPA3-Enterprise 192-bit mode", "Lower AP power and add directional antennas"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A captive portal handles web-based guest authentication and does nothing about RF coverage. It fits a guest network where users accept terms or enter a voucher.", "Incorrect. A hidden SSID is trivially discovered by passive sniffing and MACs can be spoofed, so neither limits where the signal reaches. They are weak supplementary measures.", "Incorrect. The 192-bit suite raises cryptographic strength, but encryption is already strong and the problem is physical exposure. It fits environments that must meet CNSA-level requirements.", "Correct. The issue is signal leakage, so limit the physical RF footprint by reducing power and shaping coverage; strong authentication is already in place."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A cloud team launches hundreds of identical VM instances from a template each day. Which approach best ensures every instance starts hardened with the least configuration drift?",c:["Harden, patch and scan the golden image before publishing", "Run a hardening script on each instance after launch", "Use the marketplace image and rely on CSPM to flag issues", "Install a host-based IPS on each instance at launch"],a:0,e:["Correct. Securing the source image means every instance inherits the same hardened state at launch, and a pre-publication scan verifies it once instead of per instance.", "Incorrect. Per-instance scripts leave a window of exposure after boot and drift when scripts fail or change. They are useful for settings that must be injected at runtime, such as secrets.", "Incorrect. A CSPM tool detects misconfigurations after the fact rather than preventing them, and marketplace images are generic. It complements, but does not replace, a hardened image.", "Incorrect. A host IPS blocks some attacks but does not remove weak defaults, unneeded services or missing patches in the base OS. It is a layered control on top of hardening."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A developer team's pipeline runs SAST before merge and DAST in staging. Which statement correctly describes what each does and why both are needed?",c:["SAST probes the running application with crafted requests; DAST reviews source code without executing it", "SAST needs a deployed environment to trace data flows; DAST needs repository access to build a call graph", "SAST reviews source or bytecode without running it; DAST probes the running application from the outside", "SAST inspects third-party library versions and licenses; DAST scans source for hard-coded secrets"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. This reverses the two techniques. It describes DAST as SAST and the reverse, so the pipeline order and coverage would be misunderstood.", "Incorrect. Both requirements are swapped: static analysis works on code at rest and dynamic testing is a black-box test of a running target that needs no source access.", "Correct. SAST finds code-level flaws early, such as injection patterns, while DAST finds runtime and configuration issues, such as missing headers or authentication logic errors, that only appear when the app executes.", "Incorrect. Library inventories and licenses are the job of software composition analysis, and secrets scanning is a static check, so this blends separate tools into the wrong categories."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"An attacker plugs a laptop into an unused wall jack in a conference room and immediately receives an IP address on the production VLAN. Which switch hardening measure addresses this MOST directly?",c:["Enable BPDU guard on all access-layer ports", "Enable DHCP snooping on the access VLANs", "Enable dynamic ARP inspection on access VLANs", "Disable unused ports or use a dead-end VLAN"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. BPDU guard shuts a port that receives spanning-tree frames, preventing rogue switches from altering topology. It would not stop a plain laptop from getting a production address.", "Incorrect. DHCP snooping blocks rogue DHCP servers and builds a binding table, but the legitimate DHCP server would still lease an address to the laptop. It fits a rogue-server scenario.", "Incorrect. DAI drops forged ARP replies to prevent poisoning attacks, while the attacker here simply obtained access. It fits when man-in-the-middle ARP spoofing is the concern.", "Correct. A port that is not needed should not provide connectivity at all, so disabling it or parking it in a dead-end VLAN stops the attacker from reaching production."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"An audit finds core routers accept management over HTTP and Telnet from every interface. Which set of changes BEST addresses the exposure?",c:["Enable SNMPv3 with authPriv and forward logs to SIEM", "Allow only SSH and HTTPS, from a management ACL or network", "Enable AAA with TACACS+ and role-based privilege levels", "Apply control-plane policing and disable CDP and unused services"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. This improves monitoring and encrypted telemetry, yet Telnet and HTTP would remain open and still send credentials in cleartext. It fits an observability gap.", "Correct. Cleartext management protocols expose credentials and commands, so encrypt the channel and restrict where it can originate.", "Incorrect. TACACS+ centralizes authorization and accounting, but a Telnet session itself still travels in cleartext and remains reachable from every interface. It fits an access-control gap.", "Incorrect. Policing and service reduction harden the router and protect the control plane from floods, yet they do not encrypt management sessions or restrict their source."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A user opens an Office attachment from email and a macro runs, launching a payload. Which workstation hardening combination BEST prevents the macro running and limits damage if one still does?",c:["Enable BitLocker with a TPM and require a boot PIN", "Apply PowerShell Constrained Language Mode and enable script block logging", "Block macros in internet-origin files and remove local admin rights", "Enable a default-deny inbound host firewall and disable SMBv1"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Full-disk encryption protects data on a lost or stolen device but does nothing against code that runs in a logged-in session. It fits a physical theft scenario.", "Incorrect. This restricts and records one scripting engine, but macros can launch payloads in other ways and the macro itself still runs. It is a useful layer for script-based abuse.", "Correct. Blocking macros in files carrying the Mark of the Web stops the typical execution path, and removing local admin rights limits what any payload can change.", "Incorrect. Inbound filtering and removing SMBv1 reduce network-borne attacks and worm spread, yet the macro executes locally and can beacon outbound. It fits lateral-movement hardening."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A building HVAC controller runs vendor-locked firmware that cannot accept agents, patches or an 802.1X supplicant. Its default password has already been changed. What is the MOST important additional control?",c:["Isolate it in a VLAN behind firewall ACLs; monitor it", "Install an endpoint protection agent on the controller", "Enroll the controller in WSUS for automatic patching", "Require 802.1X EAP-TLS authentication on its switch port"],a:0,e:["Correct. When the device cannot be hardened further, network-side isolation and monitoring limit who can reach it and reveal misuse.", "Incorrect. EDR needs an operating system that supports agents, and the stem states the device does not. It would fit a general-purpose workstation or server.", "Incorrect. WSUS delivers Microsoft updates to supported Windows hosts, while the controller runs locked vendor firmware that can only be patched by the vendor.", "Incorrect. 802.1X needs a supplicant on the device, and this controller does not support one. Port authentication would suit a managed endpoint or a device whose firmware supports it."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"A company requires every server to have Secure Boot, a TPM with full-disk encryption, and only signed drivers. Which threat does this combination MOST directly counter?",c:["Lateral movement through pass-the-hash attacks", "Bootkits and offline data theft from lost drives", "Covert data exfiltration through DNS tunneling", "Account takeover through credential stuffing"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Pass-the-hash abuses stolen credential hashes in memory over the network. It is countered by credential protection, local admin password management and segmentation.", "Correct. Secure Boot and signed drivers protect boot-time integrity against bootkits and rootkits, while TPM-backed encryption protects data at rest if the drive is removed.", "Incorrect. DNS tunneling is a network covert channel countered by DNS filtering and monitoring. Boot integrity and disk encryption do not inspect outbound queries.", "Incorrect. Credential stuffing replays leaked passwords at login pages and is countered by MFA and rate limiting. The listed platform controls act below the authentication layer."]},
{o:"4.1",q:"Field agents upload customer records from a legacy mobile app that uses cleartext HTTP and cannot be updated. Which connection presents the HIGHEST risk?",c:["Cellular 5G with an always-on IPsec VPN", "Home WPA2-Personal with an SSL VPN client", "Hotel Wi-Fi with a per-room passphrase and IPsec VPN", "Open airport Wi-Fi with no VPN or proxy in use"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. The VPN encrypts everything between the phone and corporate gateway, compensating for the app's cleartext HTTP. It would be risky only if the VPN were disabled.", "Incorrect. A private WPA2 network plus a tunnel keeps cleartext off any untrusted link. It would be the highest risk only if the VPN were absent and the passphrase widely shared.", "Incorrect. Per-room keys and an IPsec tunnel together protect the cleartext app traffic from other guests. Without the VPN this would deserve greater concern.", "Correct. An open network lets anyone nearby capture cleartext traffic, and the legacy app adds no encryption of its own."]},

// ---------- 4.2 (8) ----------
{o:"4.2",q:"A network scan finds 40 servers that are not in the CMDB, and several are serving traffic to production applications. What is the BEST immediate action?",c:["Find owners, assess each, then onboard or retire it", "Power off all 40 until owners are identified and approved", "Add all 40 to the CMDB using scanner-discovered attributes", "Patch all 40 in place during the next maintenance window"],a:0,e:["Correct. Unknown assets are unmanaged risk; confirming ownership and purpose before deciding avoids breaking live services while bringing each server under governance or retiring it.", "Incorrect. Several are serving production traffic, so an abrupt shutdown could cause outages. Shutdown suits a confirmed rogue or compromised system after impact is checked.", "Incorrect. Recording them without confirming owners, classification and patch state legitimizes unknown systems without managing them. Auto-discovery is a good input, not a finish line.", "Incorrect. Patching without knowing owners, dependencies or purpose can break applications and still leaves ungoverned systems. Patching comes after ownership and risk are established."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"A company will resell retired magnetic hard drives. The data must be unrecoverable, but the drives must remain usable. Which method is appropriate?",c:["Degauss with a certified degausser", "Shred through a small-particle shredder", "Overwrite with a multi-pass wipe utility", "Run a quick format and reinstall the operating system"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Degaussing erases data but also destroys the drive's servo tracks, leaving it unusable. It fits drives that will be discarded rather than resold.", "Incorrect. Shredding physically destroys the media, which meets the unrecoverable goal but eliminates reuse. It is appropriate for failed or highly sensitive drives.", "Correct. Software overwriting (sanitization) renders data unrecoverable on magnetic media while leaving the drive functional for reuse.", "Incorrect. A quick format rewrites file system structures but leaves the underlying data recoverable with common tools. It is fine for redeploying inside the organization, not for resale."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"Solid-state drives from a finance system are being returned to a leasing company, so they cannot be destroyed. What is the BEST sanitization method?",c:["Degauss with an NSA-evaluated degausser", "Cryptographic erase or firmware secure erase", "Single-pass overwrite of the full drive with dd", "Shred with a small-particle disintegrator"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Flash memory stores charge, not magnetic domains, so degaussing does not erase it and may damage the drive. It applies to magnetic media like tape and HDDs.", "Correct. Firmware-level secure erase or destroying the encryption key handles wear-leveling and over-provisioned cells, sanitizing the drive while preserving it for return.", "Incorrect. Wear-leveling remaps writes, so overwriting from the host cannot reach every cell, and data may remain in hidden blocks. Overwrites are reasonable for magnetic drives.", "Incorrect. Shredding is reliable for SSDs but destroys the drive, which violates the requirement to return it intact. It is right when the drives are not coming back."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"A company's retention schedule says delete logs after one year. Counsel has just issued a preservation notice for pending litigation covering those logs. What takes precedence?",c:["The retention schedule, deleting logs at one year", "The framework retention minimum, allowing deletion at a year", "The privacy team's data minimization requirement", "The legal hold suspending deletion of the relevant logs"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A retention schedule governs routine disposal when no obligation to preserve exists. Deleting logs under a hold risks spoliation sanctions.", "Incorrect. A framework minimum sets how long data must be kept, not permission to delete during litigation. It decides retention when no hold applies.", "Incorrect. Data minimization limits collection and keeping of personal data in normal operations but yields to a legal obligation to preserve evidence.", "Correct. A legal hold overrides normal retention schedules so potentially relevant evidence is preserved until counsel releases it."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"The vulnerability team wants to determine quickly which systems a newly published CVE affects and who must fix them. Which asset attributes are MOST useful?",c:["Purchase date and depreciation schedule per asset", "Warranty expiry dates and vendor support contract", "Installed software versions and the owner", "Physical rack location and barcode asset tag"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. These are financial attributes used for budgeting and replacement planning. They do not show whether a system runs the vulnerable software.", "Incorrect. Support contracts help once you must engage the vendor, but they do not identify affected software versions or the internal owner.", "Correct. Version data lets you match systems to the affected product, and a named owner tells you who must remediate.", "Incorrect. Location and tags help with audits and physical retrieval, but cannot reveal installed software. They matter for physical security and disposal."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"A server classified Internal two years ago now stores payment card data after a project changed its purpose, yet its controls were never upgraded. Which practice would have prevented this?",c:["Reclassification reviews tied to change management", "Scheduled media sanitization of retired drives", "Enforced retention limits on stored data", "Annual disposal audits of retired equipment"],a:0,e:["Correct. Sensitivity and criticality change as data and use change, so reviews triggered by projects and on a schedule would have raised the required controls.", "Incorrect. Sanitization removes data from media at end of life, and has no bearing on whether live data matches its classification.", "Incorrect. Retention limits control how long data is kept, not how it is classified or protected while it exists. They help limit exposure over time.", "Incorrect. Disposal audits verify that retired devices were handled properly. This server is still in service and was simply misclassified."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"A recycler issues a certificate of destruction. An auditor insists it list each device's serial number instead of just a quantity. Why?",c:["It lets the recycler check warranty status first", "It allows confirmation that encryption keys were escrowed", "It enables the manufacturer to revoke device certificates", "It matches each destroyed device to an inventory record"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Warranty checks concern resale or repair value and play no role in proving destruction. They fit warranty or RMA processes.", "Incorrect. Key escrow is handled by key management systems, not recorded on a destruction certificate. It matters for data recovery, not disposal.", "Incorrect. Certificate revocation is performed by a certificate authority against certificate serials, not device serials listed by a recycler.", "Correct. Serial numbers provide chain of custody and traceability from the asset register to confirmed destruction, so no device is unaccounted for."]},
{o:"4.2",q:"The company is buying new laptops and wants to reduce supply chain risk, specifically that unknown software or firmware could be preinstalled. Which approach is BEST?",c:["Buy from an ISO 27001-certified reseller, keep the preinstalled image and enable EDR", "Buy from authorized channels, check tamper seals and reimage with a trusted build", "Buy through the cheapest broker, register serials with the manufacturer and apply patches", "Buy direct from the manufacturer, rely on the factory image and require a vendor SLA"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A certified reseller helps, but keeping the unknown image leaves any preinstalled software in place, and EDR may not detect firmware-level implants.", "Correct. Authorized sourcing reduces tampering risk, seal checks detect interception, and a trusted image removes any unknown preinstalled software.", "Incorrect. Registration and patching do not establish provenance or remove preinstalled code, and low-cost brokers raise the interception and counterfeit risk.", "Incorrect. Direct purchase is good sourcing, but trusting the factory image without verification leaves bundled software unreviewed, and an SLA addresses service levels, not integrity."]},

// ---------- 4.3 (18) ----------
{o:"4.3",q:"A non-credentialed scan reports 12 critical findings on a Linux server and a credentialed scan reports 2. Both ran the same afternoon with the same plugin set, no changes were made, and the credentialed account has full sudo. What is the MOST likely explanation?",c:["Missing privileges in the credentialed scan hid real vulnerabilities", "Banner guesses in the unauthenticated scan caused false positives", "The server was patched in a maintenance window between the scans", "A stale plugin feed made the credentialed scan miss newer checks"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Missing privileges can cause false negatives, but the stem states the account has full sudo. It would be the explanation if checks had reported access errors.", "Correct. Without credentials the scanner guesses from service banners and often misses backported fixes, whereas credentialed checks read the real installed package state.", "Incorrect. The scans ran the same afternoon and no changes were made, so an intervening patch is excluded. It would be plausible if there was a maintenance window between them.", "Incorrect. Both scans used the same plugin set, so feed age cannot explain the difference. A stale feed would explain missing newer checks across both scans."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"Four findings are open. Which should be patched FIRST?",c:["A 9.8 on an isolated lab server with no sensitive data, low EPSS", "An 8.8 on an internal HR file server with no known exploit", "A 6.1 on an internet-facing marketing site with a public PoC", "A 7.5 on an internet-facing payment server, actively exploited"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. The base score is highest, but isolation, low-value data and low exploit likelihood reduce real risk. It would rank first if the lab host were exposed or exploited.", "Incorrect. Sensitive data raises its priority, but it is internal and no exploit is known. It would rise if exploitation appeared or if the server were reachable externally.", "Incorrect. Exposure and a public exploit matter, but the site holds no sensitive data and the severity is lower than the payment system. It follows the payment server.", "Correct. Exposure, asset value and active exploitation outweigh a higher base score, so this carries the greatest real-world risk."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A vulnerability is scored AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N in CVSS v3.1. What does this vector indicate?",c:["Remotely exploitable with low complexity, no privileges and no user interaction", "Requires local access, with low complexity, no privileges and no user interaction", "Remotely exploitable but needs low privileges and a user to click", "Reachable from the adjacent network only, with high complexity and user interaction required"],a:0,e:["Correct. Network attack vector, low complexity, no privileges required and no user interaction means anyone can exploit it remotely without help, which makes it wormable.", "Incorrect. A local attack vector is AV:L. This would describe a flaw an attacker must trigger from an existing session on the host.", "Incorrect. That would be PR:L and UI:R. Here both are N, so no account and no victim action are required.", "Incorrect. Adjacent is AV:A, high complexity is AC:H, and required interaction is UI:R. The vector given says the opposite on all three."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A hypervisor flaw lets code in a guest VM compromise the host and other tenants. Which CVSS metric captures that the impact crosses a security boundary?",c:["Attack Vector (AV): the path the attacker uses to reach the target", "Attack Complexity (AC): conditions outside the attacker's control", "Scope (S): whether impact extends past the vulnerable component", "User Interaction (UI): whether a victim must take some action"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Attack Vector describes how the attacker reaches the target (network, adjacent, local, physical). It would change if the flaw required local console access.", "Incorrect. Complexity captures conditions outside the attacker's control, such as race conditions. It would be high if exploitation depended on unusual timing or configuration.", "Correct. Scope indicates whether the vulnerable component's impact extends beyond its own authority, as with a VM escape reaching the host.", "Incorrect. UI reflects whether a person other than the attacker must act. It would be required if the guest escape depended on an administrator opening a file."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A critical application cannot be patched for 90 days because of vendor certification, and the business must keep it running. Which is the BEST documented response?",c:["An open-ended risk acceptance signed off by the application owner", "Cyber insurance to cover potential losses, with no technical changes", "Taking the application offline until the vendor certifies the patch", "A time-bound exception, owner sign-off and compensating controls"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Acceptance without an expiry or compensating controls lets the exposure persist well beyond the certification delay. It suits low risks that will never be remediated.", "Incorrect. Insurance transfers financial impact only and leaves the application exposed. It supplements mitigation but does not replace it for a known critical flaw.", "Incorrect. Avoidance removes the risk, but the business must keep the application running. It is appropriate when the system is non-essential or the risk is intolerable.", "Correct. Exceptions must be documented, owned, compensated and given an expiry date so the risk is managed rather than ignored."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A scanner reports a critical vulnerability, but analysts verify a WAF rule blocks the exploit path and the vulnerable module is disabled. How should the finding be recorded?",c:["A false positive, closed and removed from scan reporting", "Validated as mitigated, risk lowered, permanent fix tracked", "A true positive requiring immediate shutdown of the server", "An accepted risk suppressed from scan results for twelve months"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. The vulnerable software is present, so the scanner was right. A false positive means the flaw does not exist, for example a banner misread.", "Correct. The flaw exists but is not currently exploitable, so recording the mitigation and keeping a remediation ticket preserves accuracy and accountability.", "Incorrect. The finding is real but mitigated, so a shutdown is disproportionate. Emergency shutdown fits a flaw being actively exploited with no mitigation.", "Incorrect. Suppression removes visibility and the underlying issue goes untracked. Risk acceptance requires formal approval and does not apply when a fix is intended."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A security team wants the EARLIEST warning that employee credentials are being offered for sale. Which source is MOST likely to provide it?",c:["Vendor security advisories and bulletins", "Automated feeds of malicious IP indicators", "Dark web and criminal forum monitoring", "The National Vulnerability Database (NVD)"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Advisories announce product flaws and patches. They are a good source for vulnerability management rather than breach indicators.", "Incorrect. Indicator feeds support blocking known bad infrastructure, but they will not show that your accounts are for sale.", "Correct. Criminal markets and forums are where stolen credentials are advertised, and monitoring them gives warning before the credentials are abused.", "Incorrect. NVD catalogs CVEs and scores for software flaws, not leaked credentials. It supports patch prioritization."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A financial company wants vetted, industry-specific threat information shared by peer organizations in its sector. Which source is BEST?",c:["Membership in a sector-specific ISAC community", "A commercial threat intelligence subscription", "Open-source intelligence (OSINT) feeds and blogs", "A government automated indicator sharing program"],a:0,e:["Correct. ISACs are member-based communities that share vetted, sector-relevant threat intelligence among peers.", "Incorrect. Commercial feeds offer broad coverage but are not peer-sourced or sector-vetted by members. They fit teams needing wide, curated indicator coverage.", "Incorrect. OSINT is freely available and useful but unvetted and not tailored to the sector. It supplements membership in an information-sharing community.", "Incorrect. Government feeds are cross-sector and indicator focused rather than peer-vetted for one industry. They fit organizations wanting broad machine-readable indicators."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"During a penetration test, testers chain a finding the scanner rated medium with a weak service account configuration and gain domain admin. What lesson is MOST important?",c:["Base scores ignore attack chaining and local context, so prioritize using exploit validation", "Scanner ratings are unreliable, so penetration testing should replace scanning", "Domain admin access proves the scanner mis-scored, so every scanner should rate findings higher", "Medium findings are low priority, so the result is an outlier to be logged"],a:0,e:["Correct. Moderate findings can combine into critical impact, so risk ranking should consider environment and attack paths validated through testing.", "Incorrect. Scanning provides broad, repeatable coverage that testing cannot match. Penetration tests complement scans by validating real impact.", "Incorrect. Raising all severities would flood teams with noise and hide true priorities. The fix is contextual validation, not uniform inflation.", "Incorrect. The test just showed that a medium finding enabled a full compromise. Treating it as an outlier ignores the demonstrated attack path."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"Which scenario is an example of responsible (coordinated) disclosure?",c:["Publishing a working exploit immediately so users can respond", "Selling the exploit to a broker on the zero-day market", "Privately reporting a flaw, awaiting a fix, then publishing", "Testing a production site without permission, then reporting"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. This is full disclosure, which gives attackers a ready exploit before a fix exists. Researchers use it only when vendors are unresponsive.", "Incorrect. Selling to a broker is a commercial exploit sale and may enable attackers; it is not coordination with the vendor.", "Correct. Coordinated disclosure gives the vendor time to patch before details go public, balancing user safety and transparency.", "Incorrect. Testing without permission is unauthorized access regardless of the intent. Bug bounty programs define scope and rules first."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A flaw in a custom authentication flow appears only at runtime when a specific sequence of inputs is submitted. Which technique is MOST likely to find it?",c:["Static analysis of the application source code", "Software composition analysis of its dependencies", "A credentialed vulnerability scan of the host server", "Dynamic testing and fuzzing of the running app"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. SAST is strong at code patterns but struggles with flaws that arise only from runtime state and input sequences. It fits early detection of injection or hard-coded secrets.", "Incorrect. SCA identifies vulnerable third-party components, whereas this flaw is in custom code. It fits when a known-vulnerable library is suspected.", "Incorrect. Network scanners match known signatures against hosts and services and will not exercise custom application logic. They fit patch and configuration checks.", "Correct. Runtime logic flaws that depend on input sequences surface when the application is executed and probed with varied inputs."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A major logging library vulnerability is disclosed. Which vendor-supplied artifact lets you determine within minutes whether a product embeds the vulnerable library?",c:["A code-signing certificate for the product binaries", "A software bill of materials (SBOM) for the product", "A SOC 2 Type II report on the vendor's controls", "A penetration test executive summary for the product"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Code signing shows who published the software and that it was not altered, but it does not list embedded libraries.", "Correct. An SBOM lists the components and versions inside a product, so you can match them against newly disclosed CVEs quickly.", "Incorrect. SOC 2 attests to the vendor's operational controls over a period. It says nothing about which library versions a product contains.", "Incorrect. A test summary describes findings from a specific assessment and will not inventory every component. It is useful for judging overall assurance."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"After remediation, a credentialed rescan with an updated plugin set still reports the vulnerability. The package manager shows the new version installed on a single, non-clustered server. What is the MOST likely cause?",c:["The scanner's plugin feed is stale and flags already fixed versions", "Scan authentication failed, so the scanner fell back to banner checks", "Running processes still use the old library loaded before the patch", "The scan reached a different, unpatched node behind a load balancer"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. The plugin set was updated, so stale detection logic is unlikely. It would be likely if plugins had not been refreshed in months.", "Incorrect. The scan is credentialed and the stem gives no authentication errors. Fallback to banner checks is a common cause of discrepancies when logins fail.", "Correct. Patched files on disk do not replace code already loaded in memory, so services must be restarted for the fix to take effect and for the scanner to see it.", "Incorrect. The environment is a single, non-clustered server. This would explain inconsistent results in a clustered or load-balanced service."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"Which statement about vulnerability remediation SLAs is MOST accurate?",c:["One 30-day deadline for every severity keeps the process simple and fair", "Timelines scale by severity (critical in 7 days) per risk tolerance", "The scanner vendor's default timelines apply whatever the risk appetite", "SLAs are required only for externally exposed assets, not internal ones"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A uniform deadline either delays critical fixes or overburdens teams with low-risk items. Differentiated timelines match effort to risk.", "Correct. SLAs scale with severity and risk appetite and create accountability for fixes.", "Incorrect. Default values are a starting point, but each organization sets timelines to match its own risk tolerance, assets and regulatory duties.", "Incorrect. Internal systems can be reached after a foothold, so they need timelines too, usually longer. External exposure raises urgency rather than defining who needs an SLA."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A penetration tester receives a standard employee laptop and domain credentials, but no network diagrams or documentation, in order to simulate a malicious insider. What type of test is this?",c:["Fully known environment (white box)", "Fully unknown environment (black box)", "Red team adversary emulation exercise", "Partially known environment (gray box)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. White box testers receive full information such as diagrams and source. It fits a thorough review where efficiency matters more than realism.", "Incorrect. Black box testers start with no access or information, as an outside attacker would. This tester already holds valid credentials.", "Incorrect. A purple team exercise joins offensive and defensive staff collaboratively to improve detection. It is not a classification of tester knowledge.", "Correct. The tester has some access and knowledge, such as credentials, but no detailed documentation, which defines a gray-box test."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"An organization buys cyber insurance to cover the residual financial impact of a successful exploit. Which risk response is this?",c:["Transfer: shifting financial impact to a third party", "Mitigate: reducing likelihood or impact with controls", "Avoid: ceasing the activity that creates the risk", "Accept: tolerating the risk without further action"],a:0,e:["Correct. Insurance moves the financial consequences of a loss to the insurer without removing the vulnerability.", "Incorrect. Mitigation applies controls such as patching or segmentation. It would apply if the money were spent on technical safeguards.", "Incorrect. Avoidance eliminates the risk by dropping the risky activity or system. Insurance leaves the activity in place.", "Incorrect. Acceptance means choosing to bear the loss internally, usually for low risks. Buying coverage is an active step to move that loss elsewhere."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"After a quarterly vulnerability program review, which content is MOST important in the report for executives?",c:["Per-host scan results with CVSS vectors and plugin output", "Risk trends, SLA compliance and top business exposures", "Scan policy changes, credential use and scanner settings", "Patch deployment success rates by operating system build"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Per-host technical detail is for the remediation teams. It fits a working-level report for system owners.", "Correct. Executives need business-level risk and performance information to make decisions about resources and risk acceptance.", "Incorrect. Operational configuration details matter to administrators and auditors, but they do not communicate risk to leadership.", "Incorrect. Deployment statistics help IT operations measure patching efficiency, yet executives need risk in business terms rather than build-level metrics."]},
{o:"4.3",q:"A vulnerability scan of production crashed a fragile legacy application. Compliance requires periodic scans of this in-scope production system. What should change?",c:["Exclude the app from scanning and document the exception", "Rely on passive network monitoring instead of active scans", "Scan off-hours at low intensity, coordinating with owners", "Scan a staging clone of the app instead of production"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. The requirement says this in-scope system must be scanned. An exclusion fits systems outside the compliance boundary.", "Incorrect. Passive monitoring can reveal traffic but does not perform the active checks the requirement needs. It is useful for fragile OT networks as a supplement.", "Correct. Scanning must remain operationally safe while still meeting the requirement, so tune the scan and schedule it with the owner.", "Incorrect. A clone cannot reflect production configuration drift or exposure, so it fails the requirement. It is useful for pre-production testing."]},

// ---------- 4.4 (16) ----------
{o:"4.4",q:"A SIEM rule alerts when one account fails logins on 20 or more hosts within 10 minutes. It fires on a legitimate service account after its password changed. What is the BEST fix?",c:["Raise the threshold to 200 failed hosts for every account", "Disable the service account and rebuild it every month", "Move the rule to log-only mode for the next two weeks", "Fix stale credentials on hosts and tune the rule for it"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A global change would hide real password-spraying and brute-force activity. Threshold changes fit rules that are too sensitive for all accounts.", "Incorrect. Recreating the account disrupts services and does not stop failures when credentials drift. Rotation should be coordinated with the dependent systems.", "Incorrect. Log-only mode stops alerting, removing a detection capability. It might be used temporarily while testing a new rule.", "Correct. This removes the root cause and refines the rule with account context without blinding detection for other accounts."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Which scenario BEST demonstrates the value of SIEM correlation over reviewing individual logs?",c:["New-country VPN login, then AD privilege change, then large transfer", "Repeated failed logins from one IP against a single host over five minutes", "A firewall deny log entry for a port scan from one external address", "A single endpoint antivirus alert for a known malware signature"],a:0,e:["Correct. Each event is plausible alone, but the sequence across VPN, directory and network sources suggests account compromise and exfiltration.", "Incorrect. A single log source and simple threshold can detect this without correlation. It is a classic single-source brute-force rule.", "Incorrect. A firewall deny is already evident in one log and needs no cross-source linkage. It would be correlated only with other related events.", "Incorrect. A signature hit is a standalone detection from one tool. Correlation adds value when it links it with events from other systems."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"A security team wants automated benchmark scanning to verify each Windows server against the DISA STIG baseline. Which standard enables sharing these checks across tools?",c:["STIX/TAXII for threat intelligence exchange", "Syslog (RFC 5424) for log transport", "SCAP, using XCCDF and OVAL content", "NetFlow/IPFIX for traffic metadata"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. STIX and TAXII standardize the description and transport of threat indicators. They fit sharing intelligence, not configuration benchmarks.", "Incorrect. Syslog moves log messages from devices to collectors. It carries event data but defines no benchmark checks.", "Correct. SCAP standardizes machine-readable checklists and tests so different tools can assess configuration compliance.", "Incorrect. Flow records summarize network conversations for traffic analysis. They provide no host configuration data."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Antivirus quarantines a business-critical file as a false positive. What is the BEST response?",c:["Switch the policy from quarantine to alert-only on all endpoints", "Validate, restore from quarantine, add narrow exclusion, tell vendor", "Restore the file and exclude its entire parent folder for all users", "Restore the file from backup and leave the AV settings unchanged"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. A global change lets real malware run on every endpoint to fix a single false positive. Alert-only fits a monitoring period for new policies.", "Correct. Quarantine allows safe recovery, and a narrow exclusion plus vendor submission prevents recurrence without weakening protection.", "Incorrect. Excluding a whole folder creates a broad blind spot that malware can use. Exclusions should be as narrow as possible, such as a hash or path.", "Incorrect. The file would be quarantined again at the next scan. Restoring is fine, but without tuning or a vendor submission the issue recurs."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Which data source BEST detects an internal host scanning many other hosts across several subnets, without deploying agents?",c:["NetFlow or other network flow records", "Web proxy logs of outbound web requests", "DHCP lease logs mapping IPs to devices", "DNS resolver query logs from internal clients"],a:0,e:["Correct. Flow data shows the fan-out of short connections from one source to many destinations, collected from network devices without host agents.", "Incorrect. Proxy logs record HTTP and HTTPS requests only, so they would not show scans of other protocols and ports. They fit web usage investigations.", "Incorrect. Lease logs map IP addresses to devices but contain no connection activity. They help attribute an address once the scanner is identified.", "Incorrect. Scans that target IP addresses directly usually produce no DNS queries. DNS logs fit detection of malware domains and tunneling."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"A DLP alert reports that an employee emailed data matching SSN patterns. What should the SOC analyst do FIRST?",c:["Notify the employee's manager and HR at once", "Suspend the employee's mailbox to contain the leak", "Tune the DLP pattern to reduce similar alerts", "Validate the alert by reviewing the message"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Escalating before validation may expose an employee over a false positive. Notification follows confirmation of an actual policy violation.", "Incorrect. Containment is appropriate after confirming real sensitive data left. Acting first may disrupt business over a false match.", "Incorrect. Tuning is an improvement step after investigation shows which matches are false positives. Doing it first could suppress a real incident.", "Correct. Triage comes first: pattern matches often include false positives, so confirm before taking disruptive action."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Why archive older logs to lower-cost storage instead of keeping everything in the SIEM?",c:["Archived logs are normalized and correlated in real time", "Tiered storage keeps long retention affordable and the SIEM fast", "Archiving encrypts log data in transit from the collectors", "Archiving removes the need for integrity controls such as hashing"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Archives are typically not correlated live; they are retrieved when needed. Real-time correlation happens on ingested data in the SIEM.", "Correct. Hot storage handles recent searchable data and cheaper archives handle compliance and investigations, balancing cost and performance.", "Incorrect. Transport protection is a collector and forwarder setting, independent of long-term storage tiers. Archiving concerns retention cost.", "Incorrect. Archived logs still need integrity protection to be trusted as evidence. Hashing and write-once storage are commonly applied to archives."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Which monitoring approach BEST detects a zero-day exploit on endpoints?",c:["Signature-based antivirus with daily updates", "Authenticated vulnerability scanning", "Behavioral detection in an EDR platform", "Host firewall rules by port and protocol"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Signatures require known samples and a zero-day by definition has none yet. Signatures remain effective for common known malware.", "Incorrect. Scanners find known weaknesses using plugins, so they cannot identify exploitation of an unknown flaw. They support patch management.", "Correct. Zero-days have no signatures, so unusual process behavior, such as an application spawning a shell, reveals them.", "Incorrect. Port filtering restricts exposure, but an exploit abusing an allowed service passes. It reduces attack surface rather than detecting exploitation."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"A NAC solution performs agentless posture checks. Which information is it LEAST likely to obtain reliably?",c:["The device MAC address learned from the switch", "The IP address leased to the device through DHCP", "Registry settings and running processes on the host", "The switch port or wireless access point used"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. The MAC address is visible on the network and available to NAC without any software on the endpoint.", "Incorrect. NAC sees DHCP exchanges and can record the address without host-based software.", "Correct. Without an agent, the NAC cannot inspect the host internals, so deep posture data is unavailable.", "Incorrect. Connection location comes from the network infrastructure, so an agent is not needed to learn it."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Which pair of metrics BEST indicates that SIEM alert tuning is working?",c:["Higher true-positive rate and shorter triage time", "Rising total alert volume and a falling closure rate", "Falling log source count and falling storage cost", "Rising mean time to detect and falling alert volume"],a:0,e:["Correct. Better signal-to-noise and faster handling show that tuning improved detection quality and analyst efficiency.", "Incorrect. More alerts closed more slowly indicates growing noise and backlog. Volume alone is not a sign of better detection.", "Incorrect. Fewer log sources reduce visibility and costs, but they do not show that the rules are better tuned.", "Incorrect. Slower detection with fewer alerts may mean attacks are being missed. Tuning should shorten detection time while reducing false alarms."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Which of the following is NOT a typical SIEM data source?",c:["Windows event logs from domain controllers", "Architectural floor plans of the data center", "Firewall and IPS logs from the network edge", "EDR telemetry and alerts from managed endpoints"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. Domain controller logs record authentications and directory changes and are a core SIEM source.", "Correct. Floor plans are static documents rather than event data, so they are not ingested for correlation.", "Incorrect. Network security device logs supply connection and alert data that SIEMs correlate.", "Incorrect. Endpoint telemetry shows process and file activity and is a standard SIEM source."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Which tool would BEST detect unauthorized changes to /etc/passwd on Linux servers?",c:["A network IDS with signature-based rules", "NetFlow collection on the access switch", "DNS query logging on the internal resolver", "File integrity monitoring (AIDE or Tripwire)"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. A network IDS inspects traffic and cannot see local file modifications. It fits detection of known attack patterns on the wire.", "Incorrect. Flow records summarize connections and show nothing about file contents. They fit traffic and scan detection.", "Incorrect. DNS logs show name lookups, not file changes. They fit detection of command-and-control domains.", "Correct. FIM compares file hashes and attributes against a known-good baseline and alerts on changes to critical files."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"The vulnerability scanner runs weekly, and the network also has an IPS. Which statement BEST explains why the scanner is still important?",c:["The scanner finds weaknesses early; the IPS blocks only some attempts", "The IPS finds weaknesses faster, and the scanner only validates its results", "The scanner blocks exploit traffic inline, and the IPS only logs events", "The two overlap, so the weekly scan exists mainly for compliance reports"],a:0,e:["Correct. Scanning identifies exposures before attackers exploit them, whereas an IPS only blocks traffic matching its rules in real time.", "Incorrect. An IPS does not inventory vulnerabilities; it inspects traffic. The scanner is the discovery tool.", "Incorrect. This reverses roles: an IPS sits inline and blocks, while a scanner probes systems. An IDS, not an IPS, only logs.", "Incorrect. They do not overlap in function. Scans surface unpatched and misconfigured systems the IPS never sees."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"A SOC analyst receives about 400 alerts per shift but can review only 60. What is the BEST strategy?",c:["Review alerts in time order and escalate the backlog to management", "Auto-close alerts that remain unreviewed after one shift", "Raise detection thresholds until volume matches analyst capacity", "Prioritize by risk and automate enrichment and triage with SOAR"],a:3,e:["Incorrect. Time order ignores risk, so serious alerts may sit in the backlog. Escalation does not reduce the workload itself.", "Incorrect. Closing unreviewed alerts discards potentially real incidents. Aging out should happen only after triage.", "Incorrect. Setting thresholds by capacity rather than risk lets real attacks fall below detection. Tuning should be rule-specific and evidence-based.", "Correct. Risk-based prioritization plus automation focuses scarce analyst time on the highest-impact alerts."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"Why is SNMPv3 preferred over SNMPv2c for monitoring infrastructure?",c:["It moves polling to TCP for reliable delivery, which v2c lacks", "It adds authentication and encryption; v2c sends cleartext strings", "It adds GETBULK for efficient large queries, which v2c lacks", "It exports flow records for traffic analysis, which v2c cannot"],a:1,e:["Incorrect. SNMP normally still uses UDP in v3. The improvement is security, not transport reliability.", "Correct. SNMPv3 adds user-based security with authentication and privacy, protecting credentials and data.", "Incorrect. GETBULK was introduced in SNMPv2c, so it is not a reason to move to v3.", "Incorrect. Flow export is handled by NetFlow or IPFIX and is unrelated to SNMP versions."]},
{o:"4.4",q:"A company wants dashboards showing mean time to detect and mean time to respond by month. Which capability provides this?",c:["Vulnerability scanner trend reports", "DLP incident console and policy reports", "SIEM/SOAR case metrics and reporting", "Patch management compliance dashboard"],a:2,e:["Incorrect. Scanner reports show exposure and remediation trends, not incident detection and response times.", "Incorrect. DLP consoles track data-loss policy matches. They would not measure detection and response across all incidents.", "Correct. These platforms track alert and incident timestamps and report detection and response metrics.", "Incorrect. Patch dashboards track deployment compliance. They are unrelated to incident detection and response timing."]}
]);
